Disable AVIF input optimization to mitigate AIKIDO-2026-553733 (GHSA-2xp9-vwfh-vxw4).

The Next.js Image Optimization API decodes attacker-supplied / remotely-fetched
AVIF images through sharp, which relies on libheif (GHSA-g89c-p67h-r497). A crafted
AVIF triggers a heap buffer overflow in libheif during decode/scale, allowing
unauthenticated remote code execution in the Next.js server process.

This backports the upstream fix (vercel/next.js commit 409772ec, released in
15.5.24) to the compiled JavaScript shipped in next@14.2.0's npm tarball
(package/dist/**). It adds an early return in `imageOptimizer` so that AVIF input
is passed through untouched instead of being decoded. The guard is placed before
`optimizeImage()` is ever called, so neither the sharp code path nor the Squoosh
(WASM) fallback inside `optimizeImage` decodes untrusted AVIF input.

